-      <p class="why-it-matters">A search tree compares the key with one node per level, so its comparisons are at most the height of the tree. An unbalanced tree can grow as tall as the number of its keys: inserted in sorted order, keys form a path. A balanced search tree avoids this by restructuring after each insertion. This lesson uses the AVL rule, which keeps the two subtrees of every node within one level of each other, and it proves that an AVL tree with \(n\) keys has height at most \(\log_{\varphi}(n+1)\), where \(\varphi\) is the golden ratio of Proposition 2.4. Lab 1 checks the insertion step on 500 seeded keys; it is not proved here.</p>
+      <p class="why-it-matters">A search goes down the tree one node at a time, so a longer longest downward path means more comparisons. Keys inserted in sorted order make such a path: each key goes to the right of the one before. This lesson studies a rule that keeps the two sides of every node within one level of each other. It proves in Proposition 2.4 of this lesson that \(n\) keys then fit in a longest downward path of at most \(\log_{\varphi}(n+1)\) nodes, where \(\varphi = (1 + \sqrt{5})/2\) is the golden ratio. Lab 1 checks the insertion step on 500 seeded keys; it is not proved here.</p>
 
