         <p><dfn id="def-dynamic-programming" data-concept="dynamic-programming">Dynamic programming</dfn> solves a problem in three steps. It writes the answer as a recurrence over subproblems. It evaluates each distinct subproblem once and stores its value. It reads the answer from the stored values. There are two standard forms. In <em>memoized recursion</em>, a function calls itself on subproblems, and every call after the first for a given subproblem returns the stored value. In <em>table fill</em>, the values are written into a table in an order where each entry depends only on entries already written.</p>
       </div>
 
